sumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=7606ede1164df321a11fa37e03c1309bd8ec65a37d50c5dd220f3a65f910ab03&node=scalar.finite_maximum_unique_modulo_flip&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=7606ede1164df321a11fa37e03c1309bd8ec65a37d50c5dd220f3a65f910ab03&node=scalar.finite_maximum_unique_modulo_flip&relation=prerequisites","prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=7606ede1164df321a11fa37e03c1309bd8ec65a37d50c5dd220f3a65f910ab03&view=arguments&node=scalar.finite_maximum_unique_modulo_flip&assumptions=true"},"neighborhood_href":"/api/v1/neighborhood?manifest=7606ede1164df321a11fa37e03c1309bd8ec65a37d50c5dd220f3a65f910ab03&ref=sha256%3A3592e6a1f4e5ac9cf05e86d1dac98fabd5ac5f25ba57c4ce6e79c2b2f110d0db%2Fnodes%2Fscalar.finite_maximum_unique_modulo_flip","record":{"assumption_ids":[],"boundary":"Elementary finite-model deduction. It distinguishes exact maximizers at fixed size from near-ground pairs and supplies no rate, algorithm, formal replay or independent acceptance.","citation_ids":["authored_r10_finite_maximizer_uniqueness"],"evidence_ids":["ordinary_r10_proof_route_review_20261026"],"id":"scalar.finite_maximum_unique_modulo_flip","kind":"claim","label":"Finite Gaussian maximizers are unique up to reversal","locator":"Long reasoning record #exact-finite-maximizers","source_id":"authored_long_comparisons_20261024","statement":"For every fixed positive integer n, the pure zero-field SK Hamiltonian with independent standard Gaussian pair couplings has almost surely exactly one maximizing pair {sigma,-sigma}. Configurations not related by global reversal differ in at least one pair product, so their energy difference is a nondegenerate Gaussian; a finite union excludes all such ties. This does not exclude nearly maximizing configurations at other overlaps in the large-n limit.","status":"unverified"},"ref":"sha256:3592e6a1f4e5ac9cf05e86d1dac98fabd5ac5f25ba57c4ce6e79c2b2f110d0db/nodes/scalar.finite_maximum_unique_modulo